#import "/modern-iaf/manuals/layout/manual.typ": *

#idx("Four-ship")#idx("Formation")#idx("Wingmen")The fourth #term("lesson") of the Sufa: mission 2014, from Ramon, runway 25R. You lead Alpha, a four-ship of Sufas. You are Alpha 1; Alpha 2, 3 and 4 are flown by the computer. Alpha 3 leads the second pair.

=== Where the flight starts <lesson-four-ship-start>

The four jets stand cold in Ramon's four Sufa shelters: you in S2, Alpha 2 in S3, Alpha 3 in S1 and Alpha 4 in S5. The wingmen carry the same #term("loadout") as your jet. The briefing's map shows the taxi route and the route of the flight.

=== The flight's start-up and taxi <lesson-four-ship-taxi>

#idx("Four-ship, start-up")Call the #term("tower") and request start-up, as in the Take-off lesson (@lesson-takeoff). The tower's #term("clearance") is the whole flight's: Alpha 2 starts its engine 3 seconds after the clearance, Alpha 3 3 seconds later, Alpha 4 3 seconds after that. If you start your engine without the call, the wingmen start with you.

Each wingman waits in its shelter until the jet ahead of it has moved 150 m: Alpha 2 behind you, Alpha 3 behind Alpha 2, Alpha 4 behind Alpha 3. Each taxis behind the one ahead on its shelter's taxi route and keeps its distance.

=== The take-off and the join-up <lesson-four-ship-takeoff>

#idx("Four-ship, take-off")The wingmen take off in a stream: each starts its roll when the jet ahead passes 50 knots on the runway. Once in the air, each flies to its place in the fingertip formation on you and keeps it, also in your turns: Alpha 2 60 m behind on your left, Alpha 3 60 m behind on your right, Alpha 4 60 m behind Alpha 3 on its right.

A wingman that comes too near another jet pulls away from it, then flies back to its place.

=== The way home <lesson-four-ship-home>

#idx("Four-ship, landing")When you lower the gear within 20 km of Ramon, or fly low with the gear down, the wingmen go home one after the other: Alpha 2 first, Alpha 3 20 seconds later, then Alpha 4. Each flies the circuit, lands, taxis to its own shelter and stops its engine. A wingman low on fuel goes home on its own.

The lesson passes as the Take-off lesson does, once you are in the air (@lesson-takeoff).
